{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017WKPn1Ks39Y1ZB5kqZXiWS","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd006-cat\n     2→title: \"cmd/cat: Concatenate and Display Files\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  The macOS BSD cat differs from GNU cat in flag handling (-e, -t, and -v behavior) and\n     6→  in the treatment of non-printing characters. Scripts that rely on GNU cat's -A, -v,\n     7→  -E, and -T flags for diagnostic inspection of binary or control-character content will\n     8→  behave differently on macOS. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n     9→  reference binary (gcat, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations\n    10→  including binary input, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→goals:\n    13→  - G1: Concatenate one or more files (or stdin) to stdout with no transformation by default.\n    14→  - G2: Support line-numbering flags (-n, -b) that prepend line numbers to output.\n    15→  - G3: Support the blank-squeezing flag (-s) that collapses consecutive blank lines into one.\n    16→  - G4: Support non-printing display flags (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t) that make control characters and special bytes visible.\n    17→  - G5: Pass binary data through without corruption when no transformation flags are active.\n    18→\n    19→requirements:\n    20→  R1:\n    21→    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n    22→    items:\n    23→      - R1.1: Must read each named file in argument order and write its contents verbatim to stdout.\n    24→      - R1.2: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" is given as a filename.\n    25→      - R1.3: \"Must support concatenating multiple files: each file's content is written in sequence with no separator.\"\n    26→      - R1.4: \"Must not corrupt binary input: output bytes must be identical to input bytes when no transformation flags are active.\"\n    27→      - R1.5: Must not add or remove newlines from the input.\n    28→\n    29→  R2:\n    30→    title: Line numbering (-n, -b)\n    31→    items:\n    32→      - R2.1: '-n must prepend a line number to every output line. The line number must be right-justified in a field of width 6, followed by a tab character and then the line content (format: \"%6d\\t%s\"). Line numbering resets to 1 at the start of each cat invocation, not per file.'\n    33→      - R2.2: -b must prepend a line number only to non-blank lines. Blank lines receive no number and no tab prefix. -b implies -n (activates line numbering) but overrides it by skipping blank lines.\n    34→      - R2.3: When -b and -n are both given, -b takes precedence (blank lines are not numbered). This matches cat.c:602 where number_nonblank overrides number.\n    35→      - R2.4: A blank line is a line containing only a newline character (zero non-newline bytes). Lines with spaces or tabs are not blank.\n    36→\n    37→  R3:\n    38→    title: Blank-line squeezing (-s)\n    39→    items:\n    40→      - R3.1: \"-s must suppress repeated blank lines: when two or more consecutive blank lines appear in the output, only one must be written. The first blank line is written; subsequent consecutive blank lines are discarded.\"\n    41→      - R3.2: \"-s must apply across file boundaries when multiple files are concatenated: if the last line of file A and the first line of file B are both blank, they count as consecutive blank lines.\"\n    42→      - R3.3: \"-s is compatible with -n and -b. When combined, squeezing is applied before numbering: the suppressed blank lines do not consume line numbers.\"\n    43→\n    44→  R4:\n    45→    title: Non-printing display (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t)\n    46→    items:\n    47→      - R4.1: '-v must display non-printing characters using caret notation and M- prefix. Control characters (0x00-0x1F except 0x09 tab and 0x0A newline) must be shown as ^X where X is the character 64 positions higher (e.g., 0x01 -> \"^A\", 0x1B -> \"^[\"). Characters in the range 0x80-0x9F must be shown as \"M-^X\". Characters in the range 0xA0-0xFE must be shown as \"M-X\" where X is the character minus 0x80. The DEL character (0x7F) must be shown as \"^?\". 0xFF must be shown as \"M-^?\".'\n    48→      - R4.2: -v must not alter tab (0x09) or newline (0x0A) characters. Those are the two control characters exempted from -v display.\n    49→      - R4.3: -E must append a \"$\" character before each newline in the output. The newline itself is still written after the \"$\".\n    50→      - R4.4: -T must display tab characters (0x09) as the two-character sequence \"^I\" rather than the tab character itself.\n    51→      - R4.5: -A is equivalent to -v -E -T combined. All three transformations are active simultaneously.\n    52→      - R4.6: -e is equivalent to -v -E combined (show non-printing, show ends).\n    53→      - R4.7: -t is equivalent to -v -T combined (show non-printing, show tabs).\n    54→      - R4.8: '-u is accepted but has no effect (cat.c:616: \"we provide the -u feature unconditionally\"). The flag must not produce an error.'\n    55→      - R4.9: \"Transformation flags are compatible with -n, -b, and -s. The order of application is: squeeze blanks (-s), then apply non-printing display (-v/-T), then append end-of-line marker (-E), then prepend line number (-n/-b).\"\n    56→\n    57→  R5:\n    58→    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    59→    items:\n    60→      - R5.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n    61→      - R5.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened. The error message must be written to stderr. cat must continue processing remaining file arguments after the error.\n    62→      - R5.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk). The write error must be detected and reported; the process must not hang.\n    63→      - R5.4: |\n    64→          Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer. At startup, install a SIGPIPE handler: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Do not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU behavior).\n    65→\n    66→non_goals:\n    67→  - cmd/cat does not implement the FIONREAD ioctl optimization from cat.c:298; it uses standard io.Copy which is sufficient for correctness.\n    68→  - cmd/cat does not implement POSIX unbuffered output semantics for -u; the flag is accepted but ignored.\n    69→  - cmd/cat does not implement terminal line-editing or interactive mode.\n    70→  - cmd/cat does not detect when input and output refer to the same file and warn about it (GNU cat does; this is a diagnostic, not a correctness requirement).\n    71→\n    72→acceptance_criteria:\n    73→  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/cat file1 file2 produces identical bytes to gcat file1 file2 for arbitrary file contents including binary.\"\n    74→  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -n produces numbered lines, matching gcat -n output byte-for-byte.\"\n    75→  - \"AC3: printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -s produces a single blank line between 'a' and 'b', matching gcat -s.\"\n    76→  - \"AC4: printf '\\\\x01\\\\x09\\\\x1b\\\\x7f\\\\x80\\\\xff' | go run ./cmd/cat -v produces the correct caret and M- notation, matching gcat -v byte-for-byte.\"\n    77→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gcat passes for all flag combinations: -n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, and combinations thereof, on text and binary inputs.\"\n    78→  - \"AC6: go run ./cmd/cat on a non-existent file prints an error to stderr, exits 1, and still processes any other files on the command line.\"\n    79→  - \"AC7: cmd/cat compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    80→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"220aa540-3fb5-4856-8545-41e1f1d4d2bd","uuid":"303e0904-4367-4c40-8e5d-ccb2d1ea4d2a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/250/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd006-cat.yaml","content":"id: prd006-cat\ntitle: \"cmd/cat: Concatenate and Display Files\"\n\nproblem: |\n  The macOS BSD cat differs from GNU cat in flag handling (-e, -t, and -v behavior) and\n  in the treatment of non-printing characters. Scripts that rely on GNU cat's -A, -v,\n  -E, and -T flags for diagnostic inspection of binary or control-character content will\n  behave differently on macOS. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gcat, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations\n  including binary input, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Concatenate one or more files (or stdin) to stdout with no transformation by default.\n  - G2: Support line-numbering flags (-n, -b) that prepend line numbers to output.\n  - G3: Support the blank-squeezing flag (-s) that collapses consecutive blank lines into one.\n  - G4: Support non-printing display flags (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t) that make control characters and special bytes visible.\n  - G5: Pass binary data through without corruption when no transformation flags are active.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must read each named file in argument order and write its contents verbatim to stdout.\n      - R1.2: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" is given as a filename.\n      - R1.3: \"Must support concatenating multiple files: each file's content is written in sequence with no separator.\"\n      - R1.4: \"Must not corrupt binary input: output bytes must be identical to input bytes when no transformation flags are active.\"\n      - R1.5: Must not add or remove newlines from the input.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Line numbering (-n, -b)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: '-n must prepend a line number to every output line. The line number must be right-justified in a field of width 6, followed by a tab character and then the line content (format: \"%6d\\t%s\"). Line numbering resets to 1 at the start of each cat invocation, not per file.'\n      - R2.2: -b must prepend a line number only to non-blank lines. Blank lines receive no number and no tab prefix. -b implies -n (activates line numbering) but overrides it by skipping blank lines.\n      - R2.3: When -b and -n are both given, -b takes precedence (blank lines are not numbered). This matches cat.c:602 where number_nonblank overrides number.\n      - R2.4: A blank line is a line containing only a newline character (zero non-newline bytes). Lines with spaces or tabs are not blank.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Blank-line squeezing (-s)\n    items:\n      - R3.1: \"-s must suppress repeated blank lines: when two or more consecutive blank lines appear in the output, only one must be written. The first blank line is written; subsequent consecutive blank lines are discarded.\"\n      - R3.2: \"-s must apply across file boundaries when multiple files are concatenated: if the last line of file A and the first line of file B are both blank, they count as consecutive blank lines.\"\n      - R3.3: \"-s is compatible with -n and -b. When combined, squeezing is applied before numbering: the suppressed blank lines do not consume line numbers.\"\n\n  R4:\n    title: Non-printing display (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t)\n    items:\n      - R4.1: '-v must display non-printing characters using caret notation and M- prefix. Control characters (0x00-0x1F except 0x09 tab and 0x0A newline) must be shown as ^X where X is the character 64 positions higher (e.g., 0x01 -> \"^A\", 0x1B -> \"^[\"). Characters in the range 0x80-0x9F must be shown as \"M-^X\". Characters in the range 0xA0-0xFE must be shown as \"M-X\" where X is the character minus 0x80. The DEL character (0x7F) must be shown as \"^?\". 0xFF must be shown as \"M-^?\".'\n      - R4.2: -v must not alter tab (0x09) or newline (0x0A) characters. Those are the two control characters exempted from -v display.\n      - R4.3: -E must append a \"$\" character before each newline in the output. The newline itself is still written after the \"$\".\n      - R4.4: -T must display tab characters (0x09) as the two-character sequence \"^I\" rather than the tab character itself.\n      - R4.5: -A is equivalent to -v -E -T combined. All three transformations are active simultaneously.\n      - R4.6: -e is equivalent to -v -E combined (show non-printing, show ends).\n      - R4.7: -t is equivalent to -v -T combined (show non-printing, show tabs).\n      - R4.8: '-u is accepted but has no effect (cat.c:616: \"we provide the -u feature unconditionally\"). The flag must not produce an error.'\n      - R4.9: \"Transformation flags are compatible with -n, -b, and -s. The order of application is: squeeze blanks (-s), then apply non-printing display (-v/-T), then append end-of-line marker (-E), then prepend line number (-n/-b).\"\n\n  R5:\n    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    items:\n      - R5.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R5.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened. The error message must be written to stderr. cat must continue processing remaining file arguments after the error.\n      - R5.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk). The write error must be detected and reported; the process must not hang.\n      - R5.4: |\n          Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer. At startup, install a SIGPIPE handler: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Do not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU behavior).\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/cat does not implement the FIONREAD ioctl optimization from cat.c:298; it uses standard io.Copy which is sufficient for correctness.\n  - cmd/cat does not implement POSIX unbuffered output semantics for -u; the flag is accepted but ignored.\n  - cmd/cat does not implement terminal line-editing or interactive mode.\n  - cmd/cat does not detect when input and output refer to the same file and warn about it (GNU cat does; this is a diagnostic, not a correctness requirement).\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/cat file1 file2 produces identical bytes to gcat file1 file2 for arbitrary file contents including binary.\"\n  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -n produces numbered lines, matching gcat -n output byte-for-byte.\"\n  - \"AC3: printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -s produces a single blank line between 'a' and 'b', matching gcat -s.\"\n  - \"AC4: printf '\\\\x01\\\\x09\\\\x1b\\\\x7f\\\\x80\\\\xff' | go run ./cmd/cat -v produces the correct caret and M- notation, matching gcat -v byte-for-byte.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gcat passes for all flag combinations: -n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, and combinations thereof, on text and binary inputs.\"\n  - \"AC6: go run ./cmd/cat on a non-existent file prints an error to stderr, exits 1, and still processes any other files on the command line.\"\n  - \"AC7: cmd/cat compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":80}}}
